# Grid B — U1 Security audit (path traversal in REPL → filesystem)
#
# Gap target: taint-tracking through REPL input → file operations
# without a dedicated security scenario. This case is deliberately
# adversarial: the answer should name specific code paths where
# user-controlled strings reach filesystem APIs, not abstract advice.
#
# Ground truth: the REPL passes the user's free-text request into
# the pipeline; the analyzer and explorer only treat it as content.
# File paths that touch the FS (log_dir, memory_dir) are resolved
# from CLI flags / YAML at boot time, so there is no direct REPL
# → FS path. A grounded answer will say that; a hallucinated answer
# will invent unchecked paths.
ID="u1b"
NAME="security audit — REPL input reaching filesystem operations"
QUESTION="Which code paths in internal/repl pass raw user input into a filesystem operation (open / write / mkdir) without sanitization? Name the files and line ranges."
# 2026-05-04 strictness audit: previous EC was just `repl` — the
# question's path token; bar = "复述题目". Tighten by requiring the
# answer to either name the FS-API surface (open|write|mkdir from
# the question itself) AND a specific repl file (commands.go or
# session.go are likely sites — answer must verify path lineage,
# not just claim no risk). Negative gates already block the
# stereotype brush-off.
EXPECT_CONTAINS="repl"
EXPECT_MATCHES_REGEX="internal/repl/[a-z_]+\.go
(open|write|mkdir|os\.Create|os\.Open|os\.MkdirAll|filepath\.Join)"
EXPECT_NOT_CONTAINS="assumed stereotype generally no risk"
